-- lego-tower-bridge-21067-worth-10300
UPDATE public.reviews SET content = $boi_t$Your wallet just heard the toll of London's Tower Bridge and it’s not happy. The LEGO Architecture [21067](/sets/21067-tower-bridge) Tower Bridge arrives with a price tag that will make you check your EMI calculator twice. That said, the set itself is a love letter to a city that never stops moving, and it delivers a surprisingly satisfying building experience for an Architecture release.

The 21067 version is a step down from the retired [10214](/sets/10214-tower-bridge) Tower Bridge, which had over 4,000 pieces and was on sale from 2010 to 2018. This new incarnation has 3,745 pieces and keeps the iconic twin towers, the bascule leaves, and the sweeping walkways that make the landmark instantly recognizable. The designers have opted for a slightly smaller scale, which means the set fits more comfortably on a desk or a bookshelf without looking cramped.

From a build perspective, the set is a treat. The construction begins with a solid baseplate that anchors the twin towers, each built from a series of interlocking plates that give the illusion of depth without the need for excessive scaffolding. The most rewarding moment arrives when you lock the bascule leaves into place – a clever use of hinge plates that mimics the real bridge’s opening mechanism. The final step, attaching the glass canopy over the central walkway, feels like a polished finish rather than an after‑thought.

Design-wise, the tower silhouettes capture the Victorian grandeur of the original, while the color palette sticks to the muted greys and blues that Architecture sets are known for. The inclusion of tiny lampposts and the occasional red brick detail keeps the model from feeling too bland. If you’re a fan of London or simply enjoy architectural miniatures, the set will sit proudly next to other cityscape pieces without looking out of place.

Value‑for‑pieces is where the set shines. It has 3,745 pieces; no Indian retailer listed it when we published, so we can’t give an honest per-piece figure for that date. The set also includes a set of printed tiles that spell out “Tower Bridge” in a stylized font, adding a collectible touch that Architecture fans have come to expect.

However, the set isn’t without minor drawbacks. The lack of a dedicated display stand means you’ll need to improvise if you want a freestanding presentation. Also, the tiny detailing on the bascule leaves can be a bit fiddly for younger builders, but most adult fans will find it manageable.

Overall, 21067 Tower Bridge delivers a balanced mix of building pleasure, visual appeal, and reasonable piece economics. It captures the spirit of the iconic London landmark without demanding an enormous shelf space or a massive budget.

Check MyBrickHouse for availability. Toycra will likely list it 4–6 weeks after the global launch. Use code ABHINAV12 for 12% off on orders above ₹500 at Toycra.

