-- lego-city-police-train-heist-60508-19999-for-a-cat-and-mouse
UPDATE public.reviews SET content = $boi_t$Your wallet just called. It wants to know if chasing down a LEGO train is really worth ₹19,999. The LEGO City Police Train Heist ([60508](/sets/60508-police-train-heist)) has landed, and while it’s a proper monster of a set with 1,313 pieces, that price tag is going to make a lot of you hesitate. For this kind of money, you’re not just buying bricks; you’re buying an experience, and we need to see if this particular chase is worth the pursuit.

This set brings the classic cops-and-robbers theme to the rails. You get a motorized train, complete with a locomotive, a prison car, a flatbed carrying a rally car, and another flatbed with a bank safe. The bad guys have a helicopter with a working claw – classic stuff. The good guys? A police officer, a detective, the train driver, and three crooks. The whole setup includes an oval track with a railroad crossing and a re-railing piece, which is a nice touch for train fans. The train itself is Powered Up compatible, meaning you can control it via remote or the LEGO Builder app. That’s a big plus for anyone who likes their LEGO creations to move.

The build itself sounds like a solid City experience. You’re getting a substantial train, which is always satisfying to assemble. The app-guided building is there for those who prefer digital instructions, and the 1,313 pieces mean you’re unlikely to finish it in a single afternoon. The play features are standard for a LEGO City police set: the helicopter’s claw trying to snatch the safe, the rally car being transported, and the prison cell ready to hold the apprehended villains. It’s all about imaginative play, and for younger builders aged 7 and up, this will likely hit all the right notes.

However, the elephant in the room is the price. ₹19,999 is a significant investment. For that amount, you could get a couple of much smaller, but perhaps more intricate, Creator Expert or Technic sets. Or, you could buy a decent mid-range smartphone. The value proposition here hinges heavily on how much you or your child values a motorized train and the specific play scenarios it offers. The piece count is good, but the price-per-piece ratio isn't exactly a bargain.

Looking at the set, it’s a competent execution of a familiar theme. The train looks decent, the accessories are fun, and the motorized aspect adds a lot of playability. But does it justify that ₹19,999 price point? It’s a tough call. If you’re a hardcore LEGO City fan, especially one who loves trains, this might be an auto-buy. The motorized functionality is a big draw. But for the casual builder, or someone looking for the best bang for their buck in terms of pure brick value or display aesthetics, there might be better options out there. It feels like a premium set in a theme that usually offers more accessible price points.

It’s a solid, playable set with good features, but the high price means you really need to want this specific train heist scenario. It’s not a must-have for everyone, but for the right fan, it’s a big, exciting addition to their LEGO City.
Priced at ₹19,999 on MyBrickHouse and Toycra, confirmed in stock as of 9 Sep 2026. Use code ABHINAV12 for 12% off on orders above ₹500 at Toycra.
